neato!  how's this:  have ladders exist in run-of-the-mill rooms of
normal dungeon levels.  the ladders would lead to single-room attics
(or cellars).  the attic could itself have an additional ladder
leading to another attic or perhaps to a cellar (i.e.  you go up
ladder A and down ladder B and you're not back on the original level.)
the attics would not be shown as being a part of the previous dungeon
level.  golems are known to frequent these rooms and a rope golem is
always to be found there.  on occasion, the rooms are over-populated
with partying monsters who are more than glad to have you join them
(for dinner).  attics are always dark.

being made of rope, ladders are known to break (oftentimes inconveniently
after you've climbed it), however they are repaired by applying rope
(rope golems carry these) to the broken ladder. you lose the rope in
the process. a broken ladder can't be climbed! it is conceivable that an
iron chain could be used to make an unbreakable ladder ...

rope has other uses:
when wielded as a weapon you can effectively tie-down a monster but this
takes at least 3 moves during which the monster gets to hit. some monsters
(i.e. oozes and small insects) can't be tied-down although the attempt won't
be denied. a successfully tied-down monster is effectively paralyzed.

when applied to your pet it serves as a leash (leashes should be completely
superseded by rope).

when thrown there's a small chance that the rope will lasso a monster
or object.  the lassoed item can then be pulled to you in successive
moves using the #rope pull (or #lasso) command.  this works like a
fishing rod for things in lakes, as well. dexterity would improve
your lassoeing skills (chances).
other uses for rope should be possible ...

